  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Gusty winds on Saturday, creating choppy conditions for area \n  lakes, especially across northwest Montana.\n\n- Mild temperatures next week, with shower chances for most areas\n  by Thursday and Friday. \n\n- Confidence growing for colder temperatures by next weekend. \n\nGOES Water Vapor imagery this morning shows a shortwave trough \nmoving eastward along the Washington coast, with a shield of \ncirrus clouds moving over the Northern Rockies. This shortwave \ntrough is projected to move along the Montana-Canada border this \nafternoon, leading to widespread gusty winds, with the strongest \ngusts in northwest Montana and along the Continental Divide. \nWinds will be felt first across the higher terrain, with valley \nwinds picking up by early afternoon as inversions break and a dry \ncold front tracks through western Montana by late afternoon. \nValley wind gusts of 20 to 35 mph are expected, while mountain \nareas experience gusts of 30 to 45 mph. Stronger gusts of 45 to 55\nmph will impact the higher peaks of the Swan and Whitefish Ranges\nand Glacier Park. Winds will create choppy conditions on area \nlakes, especially between 200-600 pm. \n\nModel guidance is in strong agreement for a split flow weather \npattern Sunday into early next week. This pattern will be \ncharacterized by a low pressure system diving south along the west\ncoast and settling along the central California coast, while a \nlongwave trough and the Polar jet remains positioned in Alaska \nand northwest Canada. This pattern will provide mild Fall \ntemperatures, with warm afternoons and colder morning temperatures,\nalong with light winds. Isolated showers return to the forecast \nacross Lemhi County, ID and southwest Montana by Tuesday and \nWednesday as moisture begins to stream northward from the low near\nCalifornia. \n\nForecast uncertainty begins to increase Thursday into Friday. \nGlobal ensembles suggest the trough in Canada will begin to dig \nsouthward, with model guidance suggesting this trough phasing with\nthe low over California as it begins to lift northward. The \ntiming of these systems phasing remains the largest question mark.\nAround half of model ensemble members bring widespread shower \nactivity across the Northern Rockies by Thursday, while the other \nremaining members suggest activity focused further south. By \nFriday into Saturday, around 90% of ensemble members suggest a \ntrough passage will occur, with chances of precipitation \nincreasing along with colder temperatures.",
